def get_part2_raw():
    return [{'q_num': 126, 'q': 'Polar Auxin Transport (PAT) through plant stem tissues is characteristically:', 'opts': {'A': 'Basipetal (from apex down towards the base)', 'B': 'Acropetal (from root tip upwards into stems)', 'C': 'Non-polar and random in all directions', 'D': 'Exclusively gaseous through intercellular air spaces'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxin movement in shoot stems is strictly polar and basipetal (from the shoot apex downwards to the base) through vascular parenchyma cells.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Polar Auxin Transport', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 127, 'q': 'The directionality of polar auxin transport is determined by the asymmetric cellular localization of which efflux carrier proteins?', 'opts': {'A': 'PIN-FORMED (PIN) proteins localized at the basal plasma membrane', 'B': 'Aquaporin channels in the tonoplast', 'C': 'Voltage-gated potassium channels', 'D': 'Glucose transporters (GLUT)'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'PIN efflux proteins are concentrated at the basal plasma membrane of elongated cells, directing the efflux of auxin anions towards the base of each cell.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Polar Auxin Transport', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 128, 'q': 'Which compound is a classic synthetic inhibitor of polar auxin transport (PAT) commonly used in physiological research?', 'opts': {'A': 'NPA (1-N-naphthylphthalamic acid) and TIBA (2,3,5-triiodobenzoic acid)', 'B': '2,4-D', 'C': 'Kinetin', 'D': 'Gibberellin GA3'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'NPA and TIBA specifically block PIN-mediated auxin efflux, arresting polar auxin transport.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Transport Inhibitors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 129, 'q': "According to the 'Acid Growth Hypothesis', how does auxin stimulate rapid cell elongation in coleoptiles and stems?", 'opts': {'A': 'Auxin activates plasma membrane H+-ATPases, pumping protons into the cell wall, activating expansins that loosen wall bonds at low pH (~4.5)', 'B': 'Auxin directly dissolves the plasma membrane', 'C': 'Auxin converts cellulose into sucrose', 'D': 'Auxin freezes the cell wall to make it brittle'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxin stimulates proton extrusion by H+-ATPases into the apoplast, lowering wall pH to ~4.5; this activates pH-dependent expansins that disrupt non-covalent bonds between cellulose and xyloglucans.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Acid Growth Hypothesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 130, 'q': 'Expansins, the cell-wall loosening proteins discovered by Daniel Cosgrove, function by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Disrupting non-covalent hydrogen bonding between cellulose microfibrils and matrix hemicelluloses without enzymatic wall cleavage', 'B': 'Digesting cellulose into glucose molecules', 'C': 'Synthesizing pectin polymers', 'D': 'Puncturing large holes in the plasma membrane'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Expansins facilitate turgor-driven wall extension by reversibly loosening hydrogen bonds between cellulose microfibrils and cross-linking glycans.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Expansins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 131, 'q': 'The Cholodny-Went hypothesis explains phototropic bending by demonstrating that unilateral blue light causes:', 'opts': {'A': 'Lateral redistribution of auxin to the shaded side of the stem, stimulating faster elongation on the dark side', 'B': 'Complete destruction of auxin by light on both sides', 'C': 'Auxin to move towards the illuminated side, stopping growth on the shaded side', 'D': 'Direct activation of chlorophyll to bend the stem'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Unilateral light induces lateral polar transport of auxin to the shaded side; the higher auxin concentration causes increased cell elongation on the shaded side, curving the shoot towards light.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Phototropism', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 132, 'q': 'Which blue-light photoreceptors in plant shoot tips perceive unilateral illumination to initiate phototropic curvature?', 'opts': {'A': 'Phototropins (phot1 and phot2)', 'B': 'Phytochrome A', 'C': 'Cryptochromes (cry1 and cry2)', 'D': 'Chlorophyll b'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Phototropins (phot1 and phot2) are flavoprotein blue-light receptors that undergo autophosphorylation upon light absorption to establish auxin gradients.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Phototropins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 133, 'q': 'In root gravitropism, why does high auxin accumulation on the lower side of a horizontally oriented root cause downward curvature?', 'opts': {'A': 'Roots have a much lower optimal auxin concentration, and high auxin on the lower side inhibits cell elongation there', 'B': 'High auxin dissolves the root cap', 'C': 'High auxin causes root cells to burst immediately', 'D': 'Auxin forces root cells to turn green and photosynthesize'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Root cells are exquisitely sensitive to auxin; concentrations that promote shoot elongation inhibit root cell elongation. High auxin on the lower side retards growth, causing the root to bend down.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gravitropism', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 134, 'q': 'The perception of gravity in the root cap is mediated by dense, starch-filled plastids called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Statoliths (in columella cells)', 'B': 'Chloroplasts', 'C': 'Chromoplasts', 'D': 'Elaioplasts'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Amyloplast statoliths sediment to the bottom of columella cells in the root cap under the influence of gravity, triggering asymmetric auxin redistribution.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Statoliths', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 135, 'q': 'What is the intracellular receptor that binds auxin in the nucleus to regulate gene transcription?', 'opts': {'A': 'TIR1 (Transport Inhibitor Response 1), an F-box protein component of the SCF E3 ubiquitin ligase', 'B': 'A G-protein coupled receptor on the outer membrane', 'C': 'A tyrosine kinase receptor on the cell wall', 'D': 'The ribosome large subunit'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'TIR1 is a nuclear F-box protein; auxin acts as a molecular glue facilitating the binding of TIR1 to Aux/IAA transcriptional repressor proteins.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 136, 'q': 'In the presence of auxin, the SCF-TIR1 complex targets Aux/IAA repressor proteins for:', 'opts': {'A': 'Polyubiquitination and rapid degradation by the 26S proteasome, releasing ARF transcription factors', 'B': 'Phosphorylation and nuclear export', 'C': 'Conversion into glucose', 'D': 'Binding to the vacuole membrane'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxin promotes the ubiquitination and degradation of Aux/IAA repressors, liberating ARF (Auxin Response Factor) proteins to activate auxin-responsive genes.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 137, 'q': 'Why is 2,4-D harmless to mature cereal monocots (like wheat and rice) while lethal to broadleaf dicots?', 'opts': {'A': 'Monocots rapidly metabolize and conjugate 2,4-D and have protected intercalary meristems and different vascular anatomy', 'B': 'Monocots lack DNA', 'C': '2,4-D cannot enter grass roots', 'D': 'Monocot leaves have no cuticle'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Monocot crops tolerate 2,4-D due to efficient metabolic inactivation, protected meristems enclosed by leaf sheaths, and altered receptor affinities.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Herbicide Selectivity', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 138, 'q': 'Agent Orange, a defoliant used during the Vietnam War, was a 50:50 mixture of which synthetic auxins?', 'opts': {'A': '2,4-D and 2,4,5-T (contaminated with toxic dioxin TCDD)', 'B': 'IAA and IBA', 'C': 'NAA and Kinetin', 'D': 'GA3 and ABA'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Agent Orange contained n-butyl esters of 2,4-D and 2,4,5-T, contaminated with the trace carcinogen 2,3,7,8-tetrachlorodibenzodioxin (TCDD).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Synthetic Auxins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 139, 'q': "The zinc deficiency symptom 'little leaf disease' in fruit trees is directly related to impaired auxin synthesis because:", 'opts': {'A': 'Zinc (Zn2+) is an essential catalytic cofactor for the enzyme tryptophan synthase and auxin biosynthesis enzymes', 'B': 'Zinc dissolves cellulose in cell walls', 'C': 'Zinc is required for chlorophyll synthesis only', 'D': 'Zinc blocks water absorption in roots'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "Zinc is mandatory for tryptophan synthesis; without zinc, tryptophan and its downstream derivative IAA cannot be formed, resulting in stunted rosetted leaves ('little leaf').", 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 140, 'q': 'Which of the following compounds is an antiauxin that competitively displaces auxin from its receptor?', 'opts': {'A': 'PCIB (p-chlorophenoxyisobutyric acid)', 'B': 'NAA', 'C': 'IBA', 'D': '2,4-D'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'PCIB (p-chlorophenoxyisobutyric acid) is a classic competitive antiauxin that binds to auxin receptors without activating downstream responses.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Antiauxins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 141, 'q': 'Biologically active gibberellins (such as GA1, GA3, GA4, GA7) differ from inactive precursors primarily by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Possessing 19 carbons (C19) with a lactone ring and a C-3 hydroxyl group', 'B': 'Containing 40 carbons', 'C': 'Being basic amines', 'D': 'Lacking carboxyl groups'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Active gibberellins are C19-diterpenoids containing a 19,10-lactone ring and specific hydroxylations (such as 3-beta hydroxylation).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Chemistry', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 142, 'q': 'The immediate 20-carbon diterpene hydrocarbon precursor that undergoes cyclization to initiate gibberellin biosynthesis in plastids is:', 'opts': {'A': 'ent-Kaurene (derived from GGPP / Geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate)', 'B': 'Farnesyl pyrophosphate', 'C': 'Isopentenyl pyrophosphate', 'D': 'Squalene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GGPP is cyclized by terpene cyclases in proplastids into ent-copalyl diphosphate and then into ent-kaurene.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 143, 'q': 'Which growth retardant (antigibberellin) blocks the cyclization of GGPP to ent-kaurene, producing compact, lodging-resistant dwarf crops?', 'opts': {'A': 'CCC (Chlormequat / Cycocel) and AMO-1618', 'B': '2,4-D', 'C': 'Kinetin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Chlormequat chloride (CCC) inhibits ent-copalyl diphosphate synthase, preventing gibberellin synthesis and producing sturdy, lodging-resistant cereal stalks.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Inhibitors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 144, 'q': 'Paclobutrazol and uniconazole inhibit gibberellin biosynthesis by targeting which specific class of enzymes in the endoplasmic reticulum?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytochrome P450 monooxygenases (such as ent-kaurene oxidase)', 'B': 'H+-ATPases', 'C': 'Ribosomes', 'D': 'Proteases'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Paclobutrazol blocks ent-kaurene oxidase, a microsomal cytochrome P450 enzyme, halting GA synthesis.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Inhibitors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 145, 'q': 'What is the intracellular soluble receptor that binds gibberellin to trigger GA signal transduction?', 'opts': {'A': 'GID1 (Gibberellin Insensitive Dwarf 1)', 'B': 'TIR1', 'C': 'ETR1', 'D': 'PYR/RCAR'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GID1 is the nuclear and cytosolic GA receptor that possesses an alpha/beta-hydrolase fold and undergoes a conformational change upon GA binding.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'GA Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 146, 'q': 'The primary transcriptional repressors of gibberellin responses that are degraded upon GA-GID1 binding are known as:', 'opts': {'A': 'DELLA proteins (such as GAI and RGA)', 'B': 'Aux/IAA proteins', 'C': 'ARR proteins', 'D': 'EIN3 proteins'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'DELLA proteins are master growth repressors possessing a conserved N-terminal D-E-L-L-A amino acid domain; GA-GID1 binding recruits them for 26S proteasomal degradation.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'GA Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 147, 'q': 'The famous semi-dwarf wheat varieties developed by Norman Borlaug that sparked the Green Revolution carried which mutant genes?', 'opts': {'A': 'Rht-B1b and Rht-D1b (encoding DELLA proteins with mutated N-termini that resist GA-induced degradation)', 'B': 'sd1 (defective GA 20-oxidase)', 'C': 'TIR1 mutant genes', 'D': 'Rubisco mutants'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "Borlaug's Norin-10 dwarf wheat carries mutations in the DELLA domain of Rht (Reduced height) genes; the mutated DELLA cannot bind GID1-GA and constitutively represses stem elongation.", 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Green Revolution Genetics', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 148, 'q': "The Green Revolution semi-dwarf rice variety 'IR8' achieved its dwarf stature due to a loss-of-function mutation in which gene?", 'opts': {'A': 'sd1 (semi-dwarf 1), which encodes GA 20-oxidase required for gibberellin biosynthesis', 'B': 'Rht-B1', 'C': 'TIR1', 'D': 'PIN1'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "The *sd1* allele in 'miracle rice' IR8 encodes an inactive GA 20-oxidase-2 enzyme, resulting in GA deficiency and shorter, sturdier lodging-resistant stems.", 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Green Revolution Genetics', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 149, 'q': 'During cereal seed germination (e.g. barley), which tissue layer secretes hydrolytic enzymes in response to gibberellin released by the embryo?', 'opts': {'A': 'Aleurone layer', 'B': 'Scutellum', 'C': 'Coleoptile', 'D': 'Endocarp'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA diffuses from the scutellum to the aleurone layer (the outermost protein-rich layer of endosperm), stimulating transcription and secretion of alpha-amylase.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Aleurone Mobilization', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 150, 'q': 'What is the primary enzyme synthesized by aleurone cells to digest starch into fermentable maltose sugars during malting?', 'opts': {'A': 'alpha-Amylase', 'B': 'Cellulase', 'C': 'Lipase', 'D': 'Nitrate reductase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA-induced alpha-amylase breaks down insoluble endosperm starch granules into soluble maltose and glucose to nourish the growing seedling.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Aleurone Mobilization', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 151, 'q': 'How does gibberellin stimulate alpha-amylase gene expression in aleurone cells at the molecular level?', 'opts': {'A': 'GA promotes degradation of the DELLA repressor (SLN1), releasing GAMYB transcription factor to bind the alpha-amylase promoter', 'B': 'GA binds directly to starch molecules to break them', 'C': 'GA enters the mitochondria to produce extra ATP', 'D': 'GA destroys the aleurone cell wall'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Degradation of the DELLA repressor SLN1 frees GAMYB, a transcription factor that binds the GA-response element in the alpha-amylase gene promoter.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'GA Molecular Mechanism', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 152, 'q': 'In rosette plants like henbane and cabbage, long days or gibberellin application induce bolting. This internode elongation is accompanied by an increase in which cellular parameter?', 'opts': {'A': 'Both cell division in the subapical meristem and cell elongation', 'B': 'Only cell death in the root', 'C': 'Loss of all chloroplasts', 'D': 'Conversion of leaves into roots'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA stimulates both cell division in the sub-apical meristem and subsequent longitudinal cell elongation, causing the stem to bolt rapidly.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Bolting Mechanism', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 153, 'q': 'The classic bioassay that detects parts-per-billion concentrations of gibberellin based on sugar release is the:', 'opts': {'A': 'Barley endosperm alpha-amylase induction bioassay', 'B': 'Avena curvature test', 'C': 'Split pea stem curvature test', 'D': 'Triple response test'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The barley endosperm bioassay measures the release of reducing sugars or alpha-amylase activity in embryoless half-seeds treated with GA.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'GA Bioassay', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 154, 'q': 'Why are seedless fruits (such as Thompson Seedless grapes) smaller unless treated exogenously with gibberellic acid?', 'opts': {'A': 'Seeds are the normal endogenous source of gibberellins that drive fruit expansion; seedless fruits lack this natural source', 'B': 'Seedless grapes have no cell walls', 'C': 'Seedless grapes do not absorb water', 'D': 'Seeds poison grape fruits'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Developing seeds produce abundant GAs and auxins that promote surrounding ovary growth; seedless varieties require exogenous GA sprays to achieve full berry size.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Fruit Growth', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 155, 'q': 'Which plant hormone can substitute for cold requirement (vernalization) to induce flowering in biennials?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellins (GAs)', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Exogenous GA application promotes bolting and flowering in biennials (e.g. cabbage, beet) even under non-inductive, warm temperatures.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Vernalization Substitution', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 156, 'q': 'Cytokinins are structurally modified derivatives of which nitrogenous base?', 'opts': {'A': 'Adenine (aminopurine) with an isoprenoid or aromatic side chain at the N6 position', 'B': 'Guanine', 'C': 'Cytosine', 'D': 'Thymine'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins are N6-substituted adenine derivatives possessing either an isoprenoid side chain (e.g. zeatin, isopentenyladenine) or an aromatic side chain (e.g. benzylaminopurine).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Chemistry', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 157, 'q': 'Which synthetic cytokinin is widely used in commercial plant micropropagation and tissue culture?', 'opts': {'A': 'BAP (6-Benzylaminopurine)', 'B': 'IAA', 'C': '2,4-D', 'D': 'GA3'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'BAP (6-benzylaminopurine / benzyladenine) is a stable synthetic aromatic cytokinin heavily utilized in plant tissue culture shoot multiplication.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Synthetic Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 158, 'q': 'The key committed enzyme in cytokinin biosynthesis that transfers an isopentenyl group from DMAPP to ATP or ADP is:', 'opts': {'A': 'Isopentenyl transferase (IPT)', 'B': 'Tryptophan decarboxylase', 'C': 'ACC synthase', 'D': 'ent-Kaurene synthase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'IPT (isopentenyl transferase) catalyzes the initial and rate-limiting step of cytokinin biosynthesis in plants.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 159, 'q': 'The crown gall disease caused by Agrobacterium tumefaciens induces neoplastic tumor growth on plants because the T-DNA encodes:', 'opts': {'A': 'Enzymes for autonomous overproduction of both auxin (iaaM, iaaH) and cytokinin (ipt)', 'B': 'Toxins that destroy plant membranes', 'C': 'Heavy metals that accumulate in roots', 'D': 'Proteins that stop photosynthesis'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The T-DNA carries genes for auxin and cytokinin synthesis (*iaaM*, *iaaH*, and *ipt*); their uncontrolled expression causes autonomous callus and tumor (gall) formation.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Agrobacterium Tumors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 160, 'q': 'Which enzyme is responsible for the irreversible metabolic inactivation of cytokinins by cleaving their N6-isoprenoid side chain?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytokinin oxidase / dehydrogenase (CKX)', 'B': 'Amylase', 'C': 'Peroxidase', 'D': 'Phosphatase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinin oxidase/dehydrogenase (CKX) cleaves the unsaturated isoprenoid side chain from cytokinins, regulating endogenous active hormone pools.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Degradation', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 161, 'q': 'The cytokinin signaling pathway operates through a two-component regulatory system homologous to bacterial signal transduction. What is the membrane receptor?', 'opts': {'A': 'CRE1 / AHK4 (Arabidopsis Histidine Kinase)', 'B': 'TIR1', 'C': 'ETR1', 'D': 'GID1'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins bind to hybrid histidine kinase receptors (CRE1/AHK4, AHK2, AHK3) on the endoplasmic reticulum and plasma membrane.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 162, 'q': 'Upon cytokinin binding to CRE1/AHK, what is the downstream sequence of the phosphorelay cascade?', 'opts': {'A': 'AHK autophosphorylation -> AHP (Histidine Phosphotransfer protein) -> ARR (Response Regulator) transcription factors', 'B': 'Direct cleavage of DNA', 'C': 'Phosphorylation of chlorophyll', 'D': 'Opening of stomata directly'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The signal propagates via a multistep phosphorelay: histidine kinase -> conserved aspartate -> AHP proteins -> type-B ARR transcription factors in the nucleus.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 163, 'q': "In Skoog and Miller's classic tissue culture experiments, what hormonal ratio induces callus cells to remain as an undifferentiated dividing mass?", 'opts': {'A': 'An intermediate balanced ratio of auxin and cytokinin', 'B': 'High auxin with zero cytokinin', 'C': 'High cytokinin with zero auxin', 'D': 'Zero auxin and zero cytokinin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'An intermediate, balanced ratio of auxin and cytokinin stimulates rapid, continuous callus proliferation without organogenesis.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Callus Proliferation', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 164, 'q': 'How do cytokinins overcome apical dominance when applied directly to dormant axillary buds?', 'opts': {'A': 'They stimulate vascular connection development and promote mitotic cell division in the lateral bud meristem', 'B': 'They kill the apical bud immediately', 'C': 'They block polar transport of water', 'D': 'They freeze the stem'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Direct cytokinin application to dormant axillary buds promotes cell division and nutrient import, overriding apical auxin inhibition.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Overcoming Apical Dominance', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 165, 'q': 'The classic bioassay for cytokinins based on their ability to delay senescence in excised leaves is the:', 'opts': {'A': 'Chlorophyll retention (Richmond-Lang) bioassay', 'B': 'Avena curvature test', 'C': 'Alpha-amylase induction test', 'D': 'Triple response test'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Excised leaves treated with cytokinins maintain green chlorophyll and protein content much longer than untreated controls in darkness.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Bioassay', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 166, 'q': 'What is the key rate-limiting enzyme in the biosynthesis of ethylene from S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)?', 'opts': {'A': 'ACC synthase (1-aminocyclopropane-1-carboxylic acid synthase)', 'B': 'ACC oxidase', 'C': 'Tryptophan decarboxylase', 'D': 'Invertase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ACC synthase catalyzes the conversion of SAM to ACC, which is the primary committed and strictly regulated rate-limiting step in ethylene biosynthesis.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 167, 'q': 'ACC is converted to ethylene, CO2, and cyanide by which terminal enzyme requiring oxygen, Fe2+, and ascorbate?', 'opts': {'A': 'ACC oxidase', 'B': 'ACC synthase', 'C': 'Peroxidase', 'D': 'Catalase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ACC oxidase (formerly ethylene-forming enzyme) catalyzes the final oxidation of ACC into ethylene gas, requiring molecular oxygen and ascorbic acid.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 168, 'q': 'Why do fruits kept in plastic bags with an ethylene scrubber (such as potassium permanganate, KMnO4) stay fresh longer?', 'opts': {'A': 'KMnO4 oxidizes and destroys ethylene gas, preventing premature fruit ripening and rot', 'B': 'KMnO4 absorbs all water in the fruit', 'C': 'KMnO4 emits oxygen that freezes fruit', 'D': 'KMnO4 kills the plant cells instantly'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'KMnO4 oxidizes ethylene into ethylene glycol and manganese dioxide, eliminating the ripening trigger from the storage atmosphere.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Post-Harvest Management', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 169, 'q': "Which of the following fruits exhibits a classic 'climacteric' pattern of ripening accompanied by an ethylene-induced respiration peak?", 'opts': {'A': 'Banana, Apple, Mango, and Tomato', 'B': 'Citrus (orange/lemon) and Grapes', 'C': 'Strawberry and Watermelon', 'D': 'Pineapple and Cherry'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Climacteric fruits (banana, apple, tomato, avocado, mango) exhibit a marked surge in ethylene production and respiration at ripening, unlike non-climacteric fruits (citrus, grapes).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Climacteric Fruits', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 170, 'q': 'What is the nature of ethylene receptors (such as ETR1 and ERS1) in plant cells?', 'opts': {'A': 'Membrane-bound histidine kinase-like proteins localized primarily on the Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER) membrane', 'B': 'Soluble proteins floating in the central vacuole', 'C': 'Receptors on the outer surface of cell walls', 'D': 'Nuclear pore complexes'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Uniquely, ethylene receptors (ETR1, ETR2, EIN4, ERS1, ERS2) are localized on the endoplasmic reticulum membrane, requiring a copper cofactor (Cu+) to bind ethylene.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Receptors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 171, 'q': 'In the absence of ethylene, the ETR1 receptor actively stimulates which downstream negative regulator that represses ethylene responses?', 'opts': {'A': 'CTR1 (Constitutive Triple Response 1), a Raf-like serine/threonine protein kinase', 'B': 'EIN2', 'C': 'EIN3', 'D': 'Auxin Response Factor'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In the absence of ethylene, receptors activate CTR1, which phosphorylates and inactivates EIN2. Ethylene binding inactivates the receptors and CTR1, derepressing the pathway.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 172, 'q': 'Which chemical compound is a potent gaseous competitive inhibitor of ethylene action commonly used to preserve cut flowers in floriculture?', 'opts': {'A': '1-MCP (1-Methylcyclopropene) and Silver thiosulfate (STS)', 'B': '2,4-D', 'C': 'Kinetin', 'D': 'Gibberellic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': '1-MCP binds irreversibly to ethylene receptors on the ER, preventing ethylene perception and dramatically extending the vase-life of cut flowers.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Inhibitors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 173, 'q': 'Why are silver ions (Ag+, e.g. silver thiosulfate STS) sprayed on carnations and orchids by florists?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ag+ acts as an ethylene action inhibitor, preventing petal senescence and flower wilting', 'B': 'Ag+ coats the petals with metallic silver', 'C': 'Ag+ causes flowers to turn blue', 'D': 'Ag+ increases flower transpiration'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Silver ions specifically block the ethylene receptor, preventing ethylene from triggering petal senescence and abscission.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Floriculture', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 174, 'q': 'How does ethylene promote rapid stem elongation in deep-water rice under flooded conditions?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene down-regulates ABA levels and stimulates gibberellin responsiveness, driving cell elongation in internodes', 'B': 'Ethylene fills stems with buoyant helium', 'C': 'Ethylene stops all cell wall synthesis', 'D': 'Ethylene causes roots to detach'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Flooding entraps ethylene, which reduces ABA and promotes GA sensitivity, stimulating rapid internodal extension so leaves stay above water.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Deep-Water Rice', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 175, 'q': 'The commercial application of Ethephon in rubber plantations (Hevea brasiliensis) serves to:', 'opts': {'A': 'Stimulate latex flow and prolong the latex tapping period', 'B': 'Turn rubber into solid wood', 'C': 'Cause premature leaf fall', 'D': 'Prevent rubber trees from absorbing water'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethephon application stimulates latex flow by delaying latex vessel coagulation, significantly increasing natural rubber yields.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethephon in Rubber', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 176, 'q': 'What is the intracellular receptor family that perceives abscisic acid (ABA) in plant cells?', 'opts': {'A': 'PYR/PYL/RCAR regulatory proteins', 'B': 'TIR1', 'C': 'GID1', 'D': 'ETR1'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'PYR/PYL/RCAR proteins are soluble START-domain receptors that bind ABA and subsequently inactivate PP2C protein phosphatases.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'ABA Receptors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 177, 'q': 'In the presence of ABA, the PYR/PYL receptor binds to and inhibits which negative regulator of the ABA pathway?', 'opts': {'A': 'PP2C (Protein Phosphatase 2C, e.g. ABI1 and ABI2)', 'B': 'SnRK2', 'C': 'Aux/IAA', 'D': 'DELLA'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA-bound PYR/PYL inhibits clade A PP2Cs, relieving inhibition on SnRK2 protein kinases.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'ABA Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 178, 'q': 'Active SnRK2 kinases phosphorylate and activate which plasma membrane anion channel in guard cells to induce stomatal closure?', 'opts': {'A': 'SLAC1 (Slow Anion Channel-Associated 1)', 'B': 'Aquaporin 1', 'C': 'H+-ATPase', 'D': 'Invertase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'SnRK2 phosphorylates SLAC1, driving chloride and malate anion efflux; this depolarizes the plasma membrane, opening K+ efflux channels and deflating guard cells.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Stomatal Closure', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 179, 'q': 'During drought-induced stomatal closure, what is the net movement of ions across the guard cell plasma membrane?', 'opts': {'A': 'Efflux of K+, Cl-, and malate ions, followed by osmotic water efflux leading to loss of turgor', 'B': 'Massive influx of K+ and water leading to guard cell bursting', 'C': 'Accumulation of sodium ions inside the vacuole', 'D': 'Conversion of potassium into calcium'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Loss of osmotically active K+, Cl-, and malate reduces guard cell turgor pressure, causing stomatal pore closure.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Stomatal Closure', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 180, 'q': 'Why is abscisic acid synthesis dramatically upregulated in root tips in drying soil before leaf water potential drops?', 'opts': {'A': 'Roots sense soil drying and export ABA via the transpiration stream (xylem) to leaves as an early warning signal to close stomata', 'B': 'Root ABA kills root hairs to stop water loss', 'C': 'Roots store ABA as food for earthworms', 'D': 'Root ABA converts sand into clay'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Root-derived ABA acts as a long-distance root-to-shoot hydraulic and chemical signal, preemptively triggering stomatal closure before severe leaf dehydration occurs.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Drought Signaling', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 181, 'q': 'Pre-harvest sprouting (vivipary) in cereal crops during wet harvest seasons is caused by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Deficiency in ABA synthesis or insensitivity to ABA in the developing embryo', 'B': 'Excessive auxin in rainwater', 'C': 'High cytokinin in soil', 'D': 'Absence of nitrogen in fertilizer'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Mutations causing ABA deficiency (*aba* mutants) or ABA insensitivity (*abi* mutants) lead to viviparous seed germination on the parent ear before harvest.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Vivipary', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 182, 'q': 'Which manglicolous halophyte tree famously exhibits true physiological vivipary (germination of seeds while still attached to the parent tree)?', 'opts': {'A': 'Rhizophora mangle (red mangrove)', 'B': 'Opuntia', 'C': 'Hydrilla', 'D': 'Eichhornia'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Rhizophora exhibits adaptive vivipary: the embryo germinates and develops a heavy dart-like hypocotyl while on the parent tree, anchoring securely into tidal mud upon falling.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Vivipary', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 183, 'q': 'During seed maturation, ABA stimulates the accumulation of which protective proteins that safeguard cellular structures against desiccation damage?', 'opts': {'A': 'LEA (Late Embryogenesis Abundant) proteins and chaperones', 'B': 'Alpha-amylase', 'C': 'Chlorophyll a/b binding proteins', 'D': 'Myosin filaments'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA induces LEA proteins and protective osmoprotectants (sugars like raffinose) that stabilize cellular membranes and proteins during seed drying.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Desiccation Tolerance', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 184, 'q': 'The balance between which two antagonistic hormones determines whether a seed remains dormant or germinates?', 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid (enforces dormancy) and Gibberellin (promotes germination)', 'B': 'Auxin and Cytokinin', 'C': 'Ethylene and Florigen', 'D': 'Vernalin and Phytochrome'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The ABA/GA ratio is the universal molecular switch: high ABA maintains dormancy, while a shift towards high GA breaks dormancy and initiates germination.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Hormonal Balance', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 185, 'q': 'Abscisic acid inhibits which enzyme in aleurone cells to block starch mobilization?', 'opts': {'A': 'alpha-Amylase', 'B': 'Hexokinase', 'C': 'Cellulase', 'D': 'Lipase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA represses alpha-amylase transcription, preventing premature starch digestion in resting seeds.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'ABA vs GA', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 186, 'q': 'Phytochrome exists as two interconvertible forms. Red light (660 nm) converts:', 'opts': {'A': 'Pr into the active Pfr form', 'B': 'Pfr into the inactive Pr form', 'C': 'Chlorophyll into phytochrome', 'D': 'Carotene into xanthophyll'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Absorption of red light (660 nm) converts inactive Pr into the biologically active Pfr conformation.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Phytochrome', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 187, 'q': 'Absorption of far-red light (730 nm) converts:', 'opts': {'A': 'Pfr into the inactive Pr form', 'B': 'Pr into Pfr', 'C': 'Chlorophyll into phytochrome', 'D': 'Florigen into vernalin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Far-red light (730 nm) drives the reverse photoconversion of active Pfr back to the inactive Pr form.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Phytochrome', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 188, 'q': 'In darkness, Pfr spontaneously and slowly reverts back to Pr. This non-photochemical process is called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Dark reversion (thermal reversion)', 'B': 'Photolysis', 'C': 'Photooxidation', 'D': 'Bioluminescence'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Dark reversion is the slow, temperature-dependent non-photochemical relaxation of Pfr back to the thermodynamically stable Pr state in the dark.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Phytochrome', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 189, 'q': 'In Short-Day Plants (SDP), how does a high level of active Pfr at night affect flowering?', 'opts': {'A': 'Pfr acts as an inhibitor of flowering in SDPs; hence a night break of red light suppresses flowering', 'B': 'Pfr stimulates immediate flowering', 'C': 'Pfr causes roots to grow flowers', 'D': 'Pfr has zero effect in SDPs'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In short-day plants, Pfr is an inhibitor of the floral transition. A night flash of red light converts Pr to Pfr, preventing flowering.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Night Break', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 190, 'q': 'If a night break in a Short-Day Plant is given as a flash of red light followed immediately by a flash of far-red light, what is the flowering response?', 'opts': {'A': 'Flowering occurs normally because far-red light photoconverts Pfr back to inactive Pr, cancelling the red light effect', 'B': 'Flowering is completely stopped', 'C': 'Plant dies immediately', 'D': 'Leaves turn purple'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "The 'last flash rule' applies: far-red light reverses the red light flash, leaving phytochrome in the Pr form and allowing flowering in SDPs.", 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Photoreversibility', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 191, 'q': 'In Long-Day Plants (LDP), what is the effect of active Pfr on flowering?', 'opts': {'A': 'Pfr promotes / stimulates flowering; hence extended daylength or a red-light night break induces flowering', 'B': 'Pfr completely inhibits flowering', 'C': 'Pfr causes instant leaf drop', 'D': 'Pfr prevents seeds from forming'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In long-day plants, Pfr is required to activate the flowering cascade; interrupting a long night with red light induces flowering in LDPs.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'LDP Response', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 192, 'q': "At the molecular level in Arabidopsis (an LDP), what is the modern identity of the long-sought mobile 'Florigen' protein?", 'opts': {'A': 'FT (FLOWERING LOCUS T) protein', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Gibberellic acid', 'D': 'Kinetin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The FT protein is synthesized in companion cells of leaf phloem under long days and travels through sieve tubes to the shoot apical meristem, functioning as florigen.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Florigen Identity', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 193, 'q': 'In the shoot apical meristem, FT protein binds to which bZIP transcription factor to activate floral identity genes (like APETALA1 and LEAFY)?', 'opts': {'A': 'FD (FLOWERING LOCUS D)', 'B': 'TIR1', 'C': 'DELLA', 'D': 'EIN3'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'At the shoot apex, FT forms a heterodimer with the FD transcription factor; the FT-FD complex activates floral meristem identity genes.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Floral Meristem Identity', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 194, 'q': 'Which key circadian clock-regulated transcription factor accumulates in leaf phloem in response to long days to directly activate *FT* transcription?', 'opts': {'A': 'CONSTANS (CO)', 'B': 'PHYB', 'C': 'TIR1', 'D': 'GID1'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'CONSTANS (CO) protein is stabilized by light at the end of long days, where it binds the promoter of *FT* to initiate florigen production.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'CONSTANS', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 195, 'q': 'In Short-Day Plants like rice, the homolog of CONSTANS (called Heading date 1, Hd1):', 'opts': {'A': 'Represses flowering under long days, ensuring flowering occurs only when nights are long', 'B': 'Stimulates flowering in 24 hours of light', 'C': 'Kills the rice panicle', 'D': 'Destroys chlorophyll'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In rice (an SDP), Hd1 represses the florigen homolog *Hd3a* under long days, preventing flowering until short autumn days arrive.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Rice Photoperiodism', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 196, 'q': 'At the molecular level in winter annual Arabidopsis, vernalization promotes flowering by stably repressing which master floral repressor gene?', 'opts': {'A': 'FLC (FLOWERING LOCUS C)', 'B': 'FT', 'C': 'AP1', 'D': 'LEAFY'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'FLC is a potent MADS-box repressor that blocks *FT* and *FD*; prolonged winter chilling represses *FLC* through epigenetic chromatin modifications, allowing flowering in spring.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Vernalization Epigenetics', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 197, 'q': 'The stable, epigenetic silencing of *FLC* during cold vernalization is maintained by which histone modification across cell divisions?', 'opts': {'A': 'Histone H3 lysine 27 trimethylation (H3K27me3) by Polycomb Repressive Complex 2 (PRC2)', 'B': 'Complete deletion of the FLC chromosome', 'C': 'Phosphorylation of cell wall cellulose', 'D': 'Acetylation of chloroplast DNA'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Vernalization recruits PRC2 complexes that deposit repressive H3K27me3 marks on *FLC* chromatin, ensuring the memory of winter persists into warm spring.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Epigenetic Memory of Winter', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 198, 'q': 'Devernalization (the loss of the vernalized state) can be experimentally achieved by exposing chilled seeds immediately to:', 'opts': {'A': 'High temperatures (35°C to 40°C)', 'B': 'Liquid nitrogen (-196°C)', 'C': 'Pure darkness for 1 hour', 'D': 'High concentrations of table salt'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Immediate exposure to high temperatures (35-40°C) erases the vernalization stimulus, a process called devernalization.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Devernalization', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 199, 'q': 'Can a devernalized plant be re-vernalized by subsequent exposure to low temperature?', 'opts': {'A': 'Yes, subsequent chilling successfully re-induces the vernalization response', 'B': 'No, once devernalized the plant can never flower', 'C': 'No, devernalization permanently turns the plant into moss', 'D': 'Yes, but only under 100% pure blue light'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Devernalized seeds retain developmental competence and can be re-vernalized by another round of chilling.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Revernalization', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 200, 'q': 'Which of the following plants is a classic biennial that forms a rosette of leaves in its first season and bolts to flower in its second season following winter cold?', 'opts': {'A': 'Sugarbeet, Cabbage, and Carrot', 'B': 'Wheat and Barley', 'C': 'Rice and Maize', 'D': 'Pea and Sunflower'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Sugarbeet, cabbage, and carrot are classic biennials requiring winter chilling to initiate bolting and seed setting in their second year.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Biennials', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 201, 'q': 'The biological significance of vernalization is that it:', 'opts': {'A': 'Prevents precocious reproductive development late in the growing season, ensuring flowering occurs during favorable spring/summer', 'B': 'Causes plants to freeze to death', 'C': 'Stops leaves from performing photosynthesis', 'D': 'Forces all flowers to turn into thorns'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Vernalization ensures that overwintering plants do not attempt to flower during late autumn or brief warm spells before winter.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Biological Significance', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 202, 'q': 'In cocklebur (Xanthium strumarium), a single photo-induced leaf grafted onto a vegetative non-induced plant will:', 'opts': {'A': 'Induce flowering throughout the entire recipient plant', 'B': 'Kill the recipient plant', 'C': 'Cause all leaves to drop without flowering', 'D': 'Have zero effect'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Grafting experiments prove that florigen produced by a single induced leaf translocates across the graft union to induce flowering in non-induced branches.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Grafting Experiments', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 203, 'q': 'The shade avoidance response in sun-loving plants (rapid elongation of stems under a dense forest canopy) is triggered by:', 'opts': {'A': 'A low Red to Far-Red (R:FR) ratio perceived by phytochrome B', 'B': 'High levels of ultraviolet light', 'C': 'Excess water in soil', 'D': 'Total absence of nitrogen'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Dense leaves absorb red light (for chlorophyll) and transmit far-red light, dropping the R:FR ratio; phytochrome converts to Pr, triggering shade-avoidance stem elongation.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Shade Avoidance', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 204, 'q': 'Which hormone mediates the rapid stem and petiole elongation observed in the shade avoidance response?', 'opts': {'A': 'Auxins and Gibberellins', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Ethylene only', 'D': 'Florigen'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Low R:FR stimulates biosynthesis and transport of auxins and gibberellins, driving rapid internodal cell elongation to outgrow shade.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Shade Avoidance', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 205, 'q': 'The senescence of excised green leaves placed in water in darkness can be prevented by adding which PGR to the water?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytokinin (e.g. kinetin or zeatin)', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Ethylene', 'D': '2,4-D'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins delay chlorophyll degradation, protein breakdown, and membrane lipid peroxidation, keeping excised leaves green.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Richmond-Lang Effect', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 206, 'q': "Which plant hormone is commonly referred to as the 'anti-transpirant' because it closes stomata?", 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid (ABA)', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Gibberellin', 'D': 'Cytokinin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA is sprayed experimentally as an anti-transpirant to reduce water loss by closing stomata during drought stress.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Anti-transpirant', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 207, 'q': 'Which plant hormone promotes the opening of stomata in light?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytokinins and Auxins (in low concentrations)', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Ethylene', 'D': 'Gibberellin GA3 exclusively'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins promote stomatal opening by stimulating guard cell H+-ATPases and K+ uptake, counteracting ABA.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Stomatal Opening', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 208, 'q': 'In agricultural pineapples, flowering can be synchronized and induced uniformly throughout the field by spraying:', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene (or Ethephon) and Auxin (NAA)', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Gibberellic acid', 'D': 'Kinetin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Commercial pineapple growers spray ethylene (Ethephon) or synthetic auxins (NAA) to synchronize uniform flowering and fruit harvest.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Pineapple Flowering', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 209, 'q': 'Why does decapitation of a hedge plant stimulate lateral bud sprouting?', 'opts': {'A': 'Removal of the shoot apex removes the source of apical auxin, allowing cytokinin to stimulate lateral bud growth', 'B': 'The plant becomes frightened and branches', 'C': 'Decapitation allows sunlight to hit the roots directly', 'D': 'The hedge loses all water'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Apical auxin maintains dormancy of axillary buds; decapitation removes apical dominance, permitting cytokinin-driven lateral branching.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Apical Dominance', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 210, 'q': 'Which hormone is sprayed on citrus orchards to delay fruit senescence so that fruits can be left on the tree for longer harvest windows?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellins (GAs)', 'B': 'Ethylene', 'C': 'Abscisic acid', 'D': '2,4-D at high dose'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GAs delay fruit senescence in citrus, extending the market season and improving peel quality.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Delay of Senescence', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 211, 'q': 'Which hormone is used to stimulate parthenocarpy in tomatoes?', 'opts': {'A': 'Auxin (e.g. IAA or NAA)', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxins induce parthenocarpy (seedless fruit formation) in tomatoes.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Parthenocarpy', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 212, 'q': 'Which hormone is applied to cotton fields before mechanical harvesting to facilitate defoliation?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene (Ethephon)', 'B': 'Gibberellin', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Cytokinin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethephon accelerates leaf abscission (defoliation) in cotton, facilitating mechanical cotton picking.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Defoliation', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 213, 'q': 'Which hormone prevents sprouting of stored potato tubers in commercial storage?', 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid (and synthetic sprout suppressants like CIPC)', 'B': 'Ethylene at high concentration', 'C': 'Gibberellin GA3', 'D': 'Kinetin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA enforces tuber dormancy; synthetic sprout suppressants inhibit cell division to prevent premature sprouting in stored potatoes.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Tuber Dormancy', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 214, 'q': 'Which hormone triggers the rapid sprouting of stored resting potato tubers when planting time arrives?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellins (GA3) and Ethylene', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': '2,4-D', 'D': 'NAA'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellin and ethylene break tuber dormancy, inducing uniform sprouting for seed potato planting.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Breaking Dormancy', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 215, 'q': 'The classic bioassay for measuring gibberellic acid based on dwarf pea plants is the:', 'opts': {'A': 'Dwarf pea epicotyl elongation bioassay', 'B': 'Avena curvature test', 'C': 'Chlorophyll retention test', 'D': 'Triple response test'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Treating genetically dwarf pea (*le* mutant) seedlings with GA induces internode elongation to reach wild-type tall height.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'GA Bioassay', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 216, 'q': 'The enzyme ACC oxidase requires which transition metal cofactor to convert ACC to ethylene?', 'opts': {'A': 'Iron (Fe2+)', 'B': 'Copper (Cu2+)', 'C': 'Zinc (Zn2+)', 'D': 'Magnesium (Mg2+)'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ACC oxidase is a non-heme iron-dependent dioxygenase that requires Fe2+ and ascorbate.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 217, 'q': 'The hormone brassinosteroid is a plant steroid hormone that functions synergistically with auxin to promote:', 'opts': {'A': 'Stem elongation, vascular differentiation, and pollen tube growth', 'B': 'Stomatal closure', 'C': 'Fruit rotting', 'D': 'Root detachment'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Brassinosteroids (e.g. brassinolide) are polyhydroxylated plant steroid hormones that stimulate cell elongation and vascular differentiation.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Brassinosteroids', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 218, 'q': 'Which newly discovered class of plant hormones derived from carotenoids inhibits shoot branching and promotes mycorrhizal symbiosis?', 'opts': {'A': 'Strigolactones', 'B': 'Auxins', 'C': 'Gibberellins', 'D': 'Cytokinins'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Strigolactones act as shoot branching inhibitors and rhizosphere signaling molecules that stimulate mycorrhizal fungi hyphal branching.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Strigolactones', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 219, 'q': 'Which plant hormone mediates systemic acquired resistance (SAR) against biotrophic pathogens?', 'opts': {'A': 'Salicylic acid', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Gibberellin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Salicylic acid is a phenolic hormone essential for plant immunity and systemic acquired resistance (SAR).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Plant Defense', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 220, 'q': 'Which plant hormone mediates defense responses against necrotrophic pathogens and chewing insect herbivores?', 'opts': {'A': 'Jasmonic acid (Jasmonates)', 'B': 'Gibberellic acid', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Florigen'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Jasmonates (e.g. jasmonic acid, methyl jasmonate) trigger defense genes against insect herbivory and necrotrophic fungi.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Plant Defense', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 221, 'q': 'Which parameter is used to quantify the relative growth rate (RGR) of an expanding organ?', 'opts': {'A': 'Growth per unit initial size per unit time: (ln W2 - ln W1) / (t2 - t1)', 'B': 'Total mass divided by root length', 'C': 'Number of cells times volume', 'D': 'Height divided by leaf area'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Relative growth rate expresses biomass or area accumulation normalized to the initial organ size per unit time.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'RGR Formula', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 222, 'q': 'If leaf A expands from 5 cm2 to 10 cm2 in one day, and leaf B expands from 50 cm2 to 55 cm2 in one day, what are their absolute and relative growth rates?', 'opts': {'A': 'Both have the same Absolute Growth Rate (5 cm2/day), but leaf A has a much higher Relative Growth Rate (100% vs 10%)', 'B': 'Leaf B has both higher absolute and relative growth rates', 'C': 'Leaf A has a higher absolute growth rate', 'D': 'Neither leaf is growing'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Both add 5 cm2/day (identical AGR). But Leaf A doubles (RGR = 5/5 = 100%), while Leaf B increases by only 5/50 = 10%, giving Leaf A a 10x higher RGR. This exact example is given in NCERT Biology.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'AGR vs RGR Example', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 223, 'q': 'Why is water turgidity essential for plant cell enlargement during the elongation phase?', 'opts': {'A': 'Turgor pressure provides the mechanical force required to stretch the loosened primary cell wall', 'B': 'Water dissolves the nucleus', 'C': 'Water freezes the cytoplasm', 'D': 'Water converts cellulose into starch'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Turgor pressure exerts hydrostatic outward force against the relaxed, acidified cell wall, driving irreversible volume expansion.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Conditions for Growth', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 224, 'q': 'Why is oxygen an indispensable condition for active plant growth?', 'opts': {'A': 'It fuels aerobic respiration to generate ATP for synthetic and metabolic activities', 'B': 'It replaces nitrogen in proteins', 'C': 'It forms ozone to shield leaves', 'D': 'It turns cell walls into rubber'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Meristematic and elongating cells have high metabolic rates and require ATP produced by aerobic respiration.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Conditions for Growth', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 225, 'q': 'Gravitropic bending of the shoot upward is an example of:', 'opts': {'A': 'Negative gravitropism', 'B': 'Positive gravitropism', 'C': 'Photoperiodism', 'D': 'Vernalization'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Shoots grow away from the gravitational vector, exhibiting negative gravitropism.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Tropic Growth', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 226, 'q': 'Positive gravitropism is characteristically exhibited by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Primary taproots', 'B': 'Stem apices', 'C': 'Foliage leaves', 'D': 'Petals'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Primary roots grow in the direction of the gravity vector, exhibiting positive gravitropism.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Tropic Growth', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 227, 'q': 'The movement of sunflower heads tracking the sun across the sky from east to west is an example of:', 'opts': {'A': 'Heliotropism (solar tracking)', 'B': 'Vernalization', 'C': 'Abscission', 'D': 'Etiolation'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Heliotropism is the daily solar tracking movement of leaves and flower heads driven by reversible turgor changes in pulvinar cells.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Plant Movements', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 228, 'q': 'Etiolation, the pale, elongated, spindly growth of seedlings grown in complete darkness, is reversed upon exposure to light in a process called:', 'opts': {'A': 'De-etiolation (photomorphogenesis)', 'B': 'Vernalization', 'C': 'Parthenocarpy', 'D': 'Heterophylly'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Light exposure triggers de-etiolation: stem elongation slows, apical hooks open, leaves expand, and chlorophyll synthesizes.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Photomorphogenesis', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 229, 'q': 'Which photoreceptors mediate de-etiolation and greening in plants?', 'opts': {'A': 'Phytochromes and Cryptochromes', 'B': 'Auxins and GAs', 'C': 'Stomatal guard cells', 'D': 'Mitochondria'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Phytochromes (red/far-red) and cryptochromes (blue/UV-A) act cooperatively to drive photomorphogenesis and de-etiolation.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Photoreceptors', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 230, 'q': 'What is the primary function of the root cap besides protecting the apical meristem?', 'opts': {'A': 'Perceiving gravity via amyloplast statoliths and secreting lubricating mucilage', 'B': 'Absorbing 90% of water', 'C': 'Carrying out photosynthesis', 'D': 'Forming root hairs'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The root cap protects the tender root tip, produces mucigel for soil penetration, and houses gravity-sensing columella cells.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Root Cap Function', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 231, 'q': 'The phenomenon where juvenile plants have one leaf shape and adult plants have another is developmental heterophylly. Besides cotton and coriander, which plant is highlighted by NCERT?', 'opts': {'A': 'Larkspur (Delphinium)', 'B': 'Hydrilla', 'C': 'Sunflower', 'D': 'Mustard'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'NCERT specifically highlights larkspur, cotton, and coriander as examples of developmental plasticity showing heterophylly.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Plasticity', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 232, 'q': 'In Ranunculus (buttercup), the different leaf forms in water vs air demonstrate:', 'opts': {'A': 'Environmental plasticity', 'B': 'Genetic mutation', 'C': 'Polyploidy', 'D': 'Senescence'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Buttercup exhibits environmental heterophylly based on aquatic vs terrestrial habitat.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Plasticity', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 233, 'q': 'Which hormone inhibits seed germination and closes stomata during moisture deficit?', 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Gibberellin', 'D': 'Cytokinin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Abscisic acid enforces seed dormancy and stimulates stomatal closure under drought.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Abscisic Acid', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 234, 'q': 'Which hormone induces horizontal growth, stem swelling, and apical hook formation in dark-grown dicot seedlings?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The triple response of dicot seedlings is the diagnostic bioassay for ethylene.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Triple Response', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 235, 'q': 'Which hormone was discovered in association with autoclaved DNA extracts?', 'opts': {'A': 'Kinetin', 'B': 'Zeatin', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Kinetin was crystallized from autoclaved herring sperm DNA by Skoog and Miller.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 236, 'q': 'Which hormone was discovered from the fungus Gibberella fujikuroi?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellin', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins were discovered from Gibberella fujikuroi by Kurosawa.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 237, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes cell division in the presence of auxin?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytokinin', 'B': 'Ethylene', 'C': 'Abscisic acid', 'D': 'Florigen'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins act synergistically with auxin to promote cytokinesis and cell division.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 238, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes malting in the brewing industry?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellic acid (GA3)', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA3 stimulates alpha-amylase in germinating barley to accelerate malting.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 239, 'q': 'Which hormone is used as a weedicide to prepare broadleaf-weed-free lawns?', 'opts': {'A': '2,4-D', 'B': 'IAA', 'C': 'GA3', 'D': 'Kinetin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': '2,4-D selectively eliminates broadleaf dicot weeds in monocot lawns.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 240, 'q': 'Which hormone was isolated from human urine by Kögl and Haagen-Smit in 1934?', 'opts': {'A': 'Auxin (Heteroauxin / IAA)', 'B': 'Gibberellin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Kögl and Haagen-Smit isolated indole-3-acetic acid (heteroauxin) from human pellagra urine.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin History', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 241, 'q': 'Which hormone is an adenine derivative with an isoprenoid side chain isolated from corn endosperm?', 'opts': {'A': 'Zeatin', 'B': 'Kinetin', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Zeatin is a natural cytokinin isolated from Zea mays grains.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 242, 'q': 'Which hormone induces bolting in cabbage and beet?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellin', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins induce rapid internode elongation and bolting in rosette plants.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 243, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes female flower formation in monoecious cucumbers to boost fruit production?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Gibberellin', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene increases the proportion of female (pistillate) flowers in cucumbers.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 244, 'q': 'Which hormone is commercially sold as Ethephon to ripen apples and tomatoes?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene source (Ethephon)', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethephon releases ethylene gas inside tissues to promote fruit ripening.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethephon', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 245, 'q': 'Which hormone delays leaf senescence and maintains green chlorophyll (Richmond-Lang effect)?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytokinin', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Ethylene', 'D': 'Auxin alone'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins retard senescence and preserve chlorophyll in detached leaves.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 246, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes root initiation in nursery stem cuttings?', 'opts': {'A': 'Auxin (IBA and NAA)', 'B': 'Ethylene', 'C': 'Gibberellin', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'IBA and NAA are used in rooting powders to stimulate adventitious root formation in stem cuttings.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 247, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes fruit thinning in cotton, cherry, and walnut trees?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene (Ethephon)', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene promotes flower and fruit thinning to prevent excessive fruit load.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 248, 'q': 'Which hormone is considered an antagonist of gibberellin in seed germination?', 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA prevents germination and antagonizes GA-induced alpha-amylase synthesis.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'ABA vs GA', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 249, 'q': 'Which hormone shortens the juvenile vegetative period in conifers, promoting early seed production?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellin', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins accelerate cone and seed production in juvenile conifers.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 250, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes rapid elongation of internodes in deep-water rice to avoid drowning?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Cytokinin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene accumulation triggers rapid internodal elongation in submerged deep-water rice.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Medium', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}]
